1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,644
Imagine waking up alone in the Amazon. Trees taller

2
00:00:03,644 --> 00:00:06,787
than buildings block out the sky. The air is

3
00:00:06,787 --> 00:00:10,645
hot and wet. Every direction looks the same. Somewhere

4
00:00:10,645 --> 00:00:14,288
nearby, something is moving in the leaves. You have

5
00:00:14,288 --> 00:00:17,217
no map, no phone signal and no idea which

6
00:00:17,217 --> 00:00:20,218
way to go. Could you get out alive? Today,

7
00:00:20,218 --> 00:00:23,647
we'll find out, with the help of some incredible

8
00:00:23,647 --> 00:00:24,576
true stories.

9
00:00:25,126 --> 00:00:28,668
Welcome to Earth Decoded. If you love the most

10
00:00:28,668 --> 00:00:33,057
extreme places on our planet, tap subscribe. Today, we're

11
00:00:33,057 --> 00:00:36,753
getting lost in the biggest rainforest on Earth.

12
00:00:37,303 --> 00:00:41,010
The Amazon rainforest covers about five and a half

13
00:00:41,010 --> 00:00:45,534
million square kilometers across nine countries, with most of

14
00:00:45,534 --> 00:00:49,093
it in Brazil. It's the largest rainforest on the

15
00:00:49,093 --> 00:00:49,612
planet.

16
00:00:50,162 --> 00:00:54,372
Scientists estimate it has around three hundred ninety billion

17
00:00:54,372 --> 00:00:57,970
trees, from about sixteen thousand different species.

18
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:02,248
About one in ten known species on Earth lives

19
00:01:02,248 --> 00:01:07,053
in the Amazon: jaguars, pink river dolphins, sloths, giant

20
00:01:07,053 --> 00:01:11,277
otters, thousands of birds and millions of insects.

21
00:01:11,827 --> 00:01:15,316
The forest has layers, like floors in a building.

22
00:01:15,316 --> 00:01:18,449
The emergent trees poke out on top, then the

23
00:01:18,449 --> 00:01:22,365
thick canopy, then the dark understory, and finally the

24
00:01:22,365 --> 00:01:25,285
forest floor. Most of the life is high up

25
00:01:25,285 --> 00:01:28,275
in the canopy, not where you'd be walking.

26
00:01:28,825 --> 00:01:32,229
It's often called the lungs of the planet. In

27
00:01:32,229 --> 00:01:35,707
reality, the forest uses most of the oxygen it

28
00:01:35,707 --> 00:01:39,564
makes. But it stores enormous amounts of carbon and

29
00:01:39,564 --> 00:01:43,119
helps create rain far beyond the forest itself.

30
00:01:43,669 --> 00:01:46,708
Quick question. Would you rather be lost in the

31
00:01:46,708 --> 00:01:49,231
jungle or in the desert? Tell me in the

32
00:01:49,231 --> 00:01:49,813
comments.

33
00:01:50,363 --> 00:01:54,004
So why would you struggle to survive? Problem one:

34
00:01:54,004 --> 00:01:57,936
getting lost. Under the canopy, only about two percent

35
00:01:57,936 --> 00:02:01,650
of the sunlight reaches the ground. You often can't

36
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:05,073
see the sun, the horizon, or anything more than

37
00:02:05,073 --> 00:02:06,384
a few meters away.

38
00:02:06,934 --> 00:02:10,124
People lost in the jungle often walk in circles

39
00:02:10,124 --> 00:02:13,449
without realizing it. A few hundred meters from a

40
00:02:13,449 --> 00:02:16,299
path can be enough to never find it again.

41
00:02:16,849 --> 00:02:20,328
Night falls fast near the equator, around six in

42
00:02:20,328 --> 00:02:24,023
the evening all year. Under the trees, the darkness

43
00:02:24,023 --> 00:02:27,357
is total, and the forest fills with the sounds

44
00:02:27,357 --> 00:02:30,545
of frogs, insects and animals you can't see.

45
00:02:31,095 --> 00:02:35,581
Problem two: the heat and humidity. Temperatures stay around

46
00:02:35,581 --> 00:02:38,722
thirty degrees, and the air is so wet that

47
00:02:38,722 --> 00:02:42,834
your sweat can't evaporate well, so your body struggles

48
00:02:42,834 --> 00:02:46,572
to cool down. Clothes never dry. Cuts get infected

49
00:02:46,572 --> 00:02:46,946
fast.

50
00:02:47,496 --> 00:02:50,374
And it rains a lot, often two to three

51
00:02:50,374 --> 00:02:54,085
meters a year. Storms can flood rivers and trails

52
00:02:54,085 --> 00:02:55,069
within hours.

53
00:02:55,619 --> 00:03:00,082
Problem three: water. It sounds strange, because there's water

54
00:03:00,082 --> 00:03:04,256
everywhere. But river and puddle water can carry parasites

55
00:03:04,256 --> 00:03:08,430
and bacteria. Drinking it untreated can make you seriously

56
00:03:08,430 --> 00:03:08,718
ill.

57
00:03:09,268 --> 00:03:12,376
And food is hard to find. Many fruits and

58
00:03:12,376 --> 00:03:16,165
plants are toxic, and most animals are hidden high

59
00:03:16,165 --> 00:03:18,591
in the canopy, far out of reach.

60
00:03:19,141 --> 00:03:22,637
At the same time, the rainforest is a pharmacy.

61
00:03:22,637 --> 00:03:27,175
Many medicines have roots in rainforest plants, like quinine,

62
00:03:27,175 --> 00:03:30,523
which came from the bark of the cinchona tree

63
00:03:30,523 --> 00:03:33,797
and was used to fight malaria for centuries.

64
00:03:34,347 --> 00:03:39,060
Problem four: the small things. Mosquitoes can carry malaria,

65
00:03:39,060 --> 00:03:42,770
dengue and yellow fever. For most people lost in

66
00:03:42,770 --> 00:03:46,942
the jungle, insects and disease are far more dangerous

67
00:03:46,942 --> 00:03:48,256
than big animals.

68
00:03:48,806 --> 00:03:52,442
Then there's the bullet ant, named because its sting

69
00:03:52,442 --> 00:03:55,449
is said to feel like being shot. It's often

70
00:03:55,449 --> 00:03:58,666
ranked as the most painful insect sting in the

71
00:03:58,666 --> 00:04:01,393
world, and the pain can last for hours.

72
00:04:01,943 --> 00:04:06,170
Some poison frogs, with their bright colors, warn predators

73
00:04:06,170 --> 00:04:09,537
not to eat them. Their skin contains toxins, so

74
00:04:09,537 --> 00:04:11,543
it's best not to touch them.

75
00:04:12,093 --> 00:04:15,761
At night, the jungle floor comes alive with spiders,

76
00:04:15,761 --> 00:04:19,993
scorpions and centipedes. Locals shake out their boots every

77
00:04:19,993 --> 00:04:23,591
morning before putting them on, a simple habit that

78
00:04:23,591 --> 00:04:26,130
prevents a lot of painful surprises.

79
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:29,863
And then there are the plants. Some trees have

80
00:04:29,863 --> 00:04:33,529
spines on their trunks, some leaves cause rashes, and

81
00:04:33,529 --> 00:04:36,781
some vines are strong enough to trap your feet.

82
00:04:36,781 --> 00:04:39,203
Every step off the trail is a risk.

83
00:04:39,753 --> 00:04:43,842
In the rivers live black caimans, piranhas and electric

84
00:04:43,842 --> 00:04:47,856
eels that can deliver shocks of several hundred volts.

85
00:04:47,856 --> 00:04:51,499
And the green anaconda, the heaviest snake in the

86
00:04:51,499 --> 00:04:54,473
world, can weigh around a hundred kilos.

87
00:04:55,023 --> 00:04:58,831
Jaguars live here too, but they almost always avoid

88
00:04:58,831 --> 00:05:02,863
people. Being attacked is far less likely than getting

89
00:05:02,863 --> 00:05:03,833
sick or lost.

90
00:05:04,383 --> 00:05:07,625
Which Amazon animal scares you most? Tell me in

91
00:05:07,625 --> 00:05:08,522
the comments.

92
00:05:09,072 --> 00:05:12,934
Now, the true stories. On Christmas Eve, 1971, a

93
00:05:12,934 --> 00:05:16,796
plane flying over the Peruvian Amazon was hit by

94
00:05:16,796 --> 00:05:21,945
lightning and broke apart in the air. Seventeen-year-old Juliane

95
00:05:21,945 --> 00:05:26,611
Koepcke fell about three kilometers, still strapped to her

96
00:05:26,611 --> 00:05:27,013
seat.

97
00:05:27,563 --> 00:05:31,948
Incredibly, she survived with a broken collarbone and cuts.

98
00:05:31,948 --> 00:05:35,738
She was the only survivor. Her father, a biologist,

99
00:05:35,738 --> 00:05:39,082
had taught her one rule: if you're lost, find

100
00:05:39,082 --> 00:05:43,170
water and follow it downstream, because streams lead to

101
00:05:43,170 --> 00:05:45,697
rivers, and rivers lead to people.

102
00:05:46,247 --> 00:05:50,036
The Amazon River itself is the lifeline. It carries

103
00:05:50,036 --> 00:05:53,379
more water than any other river on Earth, and

104
00:05:53,379 --> 00:05:56,574
for most of the forest, rivers are the only

105
00:05:56,574 --> 00:05:57,020
roads.

106
00:05:57,570 --> 00:06:01,518
For eleven days, she walked and waded downstream, injured

107
00:06:01,518 --> 00:06:04,842
and alone, until she found a shelter where local

108
00:06:04,842 --> 00:06:07,682
workers found her and took her to safety.

109
00:06:08,232 --> 00:06:12,509
In 2023, it happened again. A small plane crashed

110
00:06:12,509 --> 00:06:18,183
in the Colombian Amazon. Four Indigenous children, aged thirteen,

111
00:06:18,183 --> 00:06:21,587
nine, four and one, survived the crash.

112
00:06:22,137 --> 00:06:25,540
They spent forty days in the jungle before rescuers

113
00:06:25,540 --> 00:06:29,543
and Indigenous volunteers found them. The oldest sister used

114
00:06:29,543 --> 00:06:32,813
what she had learned from her community about the

115
00:06:32,813 --> 00:06:36,016
forest: which fruits and seeds were safe to eat,

116
00:06:36,016 --> 00:06:37,283
and how to shelter.

117
00:06:37,833 --> 00:06:41,195
Both stories show the same thing: in the Amazon,

118
00:06:41,195 --> 00:06:46,237
knowledge is survival. Indigenous peoples, hundreds of different groups,

119
00:06:46,237 --> 00:06:49,668
have lived in this forest for thousands of years.

120
00:06:49,668 --> 00:06:53,030
Some still live with no contact with the outside

121
00:06:53,030 --> 00:06:53,940
world at all.

122
00:06:54,490 --> 00:06:57,857
Survival experts add a few more rules. Sleep off

123
00:06:57,857 --> 00:07:00,592
the ground, in a hammock if you can, to

124
00:07:00,592 --> 00:07:04,029
avoid ants, snakes and floods. Keep your feet dry

125
00:07:04,029 --> 00:07:08,588
when possible. And make yourself visible: rescuers searching from

126
00:07:08,588 --> 00:07:12,516
the air look for clearings, riverbanks, smoke and bright

127
00:07:12,516 --> 00:07:13,007
colors.

128
00:07:13,557 --> 00:07:17,887
But the forest is under threat. Scientists estimate that

129
00:07:17,887 --> 00:07:21,366
around a fifth of the Amazon has been cleared

130
00:07:21,366 --> 00:07:25,696
since the 1970s, mostly for cattle ranching and farming.

131
00:07:26,246 --> 00:07:29,598
Some researchers warn that if too much is lost,

132
00:07:29,598 --> 00:07:33,236
parts of the rainforest could reach a tipping point

133
00:07:33,236 --> 00:07:35,590
and slowly turn into dry savanna.

134
00:07:36,140 --> 00:07:39,518
So, could you survive being lost in the Amazon?

135
00:07:39,518 --> 00:07:43,472
Alone, without knowledge or supplies, the odds are low.

136
00:07:43,472 --> 00:07:47,353
But follow the water, stay calm, protect yourself from

137
00:07:47,353 --> 00:07:50,803
insects, and learn from the people who call this

138
00:07:50,803 --> 00:07:53,463
forest home, and you'd have a chance.

139
00:07:54,013 --> 00:07:57,891
Last question. Which extreme place should we explore next?

140
00:07:57,891 --> 00:07:59,495
Tell me in the comments.

141
00:08:00,045 --> 00:08:03,786
Thanks for watching Earth Decoded. If you enjoyed this,

142
00:08:03,786 --> 00:08:06,914
like the video, subscribe, and share it with a

143
00:08:06,914 --> 00:08:09,907
friend who loves nature. See you in the next

144
00:08:09,907 --> 00:08:10,179
one.

